[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Discriminated Unions and Exhaustiveness Checking in TypeScript"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript provides powerful type system features that help catch bugs at compile-time. Two of the most effective tools for modeling complex state are Discriminated Unions and Exhaustiveness Checking."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"What is a Discriminated Union?"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"A discriminated union (also called a tagged union or algebraic data type) is a type that may be one of several different types, each containing a common literal property (the discriminant) that TypeScript uses to narrow down the exact type within a conditional block."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"type Circle = {
  kind: "circle";
  radius: number;
};

type Square = {
  kind: "square";
  sideLength: number;
};

type Shape = Circle | Square;"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Exhaustiveness Checking with never"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Exhaustiveness checking ensures that all possible variants of a union type are handled in a conditional statement, typically a switch-case. By leveraging the never type, the TypeScript compiler will throw an error if a new union member is added later and left unhandled."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"function getArea(shape: Shape): number {
  switch (shape.kind) {
    case "circle":
      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;
    case "square":
      return shape.sideLength ** 2;
    default:
      const _exhaustiveCheck: never = shape;
      return _exhaustiveCheck;
  }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The discriminant property (like "kind") must be a literal type."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TypeScript narrows the type automatically inside each case block."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The "never" type guarantees that the default case is unreachable unless a new union type is introduced."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Using discriminated unions alongside exhaustive checks with 'never' makes refactoring safer and prevents runtime errors caused by unhandled state variants."}]
